---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import camps from '../data/tribe_camps.json';

// Mechanic transcribed from Tile.doTurn → skipImprovementUnitTurns /
// resetImprovementUnitTurns / countTribeAvailableUnits (reference/Source —
// all drift-watched in verify_source_constants.py). XML-derived numbers
// (tribe_camps.json, build_tribes.py): settlement tiers from improvement.xml,
// per-Tribal-Level knobs from tribeLevel.xml.
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const range = camps.levels[0]?.maxUnitsRange ?? 3;
---

<Base
  title="Camp Unit Spawning"
  active="camp-spawning"
  pageMark="🏕️"
  description={`When tribal camps in Old World spawn units and when they don't: the spawn countdown (${camps.baseUnitTurns} turns base, ${camps.levels.map((l: any) => l.interval).join('/')} by Tribal Level), and the pause rule — the countdown freezes while enough of the tribe's units stand within ${range} tiles, so killing units near a camp un-freezes production rather than speeding it up. Transcribed from Tile.cs in the game source.`}
  pageStats={[
    { label: 'base interval', value: `${camps.baseUnitTurns}t` },
    { label: 'camp tiers', value: camps.settlements.length },
    { label: 'count radius', value: `${range} tiles` },
  ]}
>
  <p class="lede">
    Every tribal settlement runs a <b>spawn countdown</b>. Each turn the
    countdown ticks down <em>only if the camp is not already "full"</em> —
    when it reaches zero, a unit appears and the countdown resets. The
    practical upshot: a quiet camp isn't dormant, it's <em>capped</em>, and
    thinning the units around it restarts the flow. Transcribed from
    <code>Tile.skipImprovementUnitTurns</code> /
    <code>resetImprovementUnitTurns</code> in the game source; numbers from
    <code>improvement.xml</code> and <code>tribeLevel.xml</code>.
  </p>

  <section class="cs-section">
    <h2 class="cs-h2">The pause rule</h2>
    <div class="cs-rule">
      A camp is "full" — and its countdown <em>frozen</em> — while the
      tribe's units within <b>{range} tiles</b> (not counting units out
      raiding) reach the cap:
      <div class="cs-formula">cap = camp tier (+1…+4) + Tribal Level bonus (+0…+4)</div>
      <ul class="cs-rule__mods">
        <li><b>Halved</b> (rounded up) if no raidable city is within the tribe's raid range</li>
        <li>Never more than a third of the landmass's tiles (small islands cap low)</li>
      </ul>
      <b>Killing units near a camp doesn't speed the timer up — it
      un-freezes it.</b> Once the nearby count drops below the cap, the
      countdown resumes from where it stopped.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section">
    <h2 class="cs-h2">Reading the two timers on a camp</h2>
    <p class="cs-body">
      A camp shows two counters: the <b>next-unit timer</b> and, beneath it,
      the <b>develop timer</b> (time until the camp upgrades to its next tier).
    </p>
    <ul class="cs-notes">
      <li>
        <b>The next-unit timer never skips.</b> The only things that ever touch
        it in code are <b>−1 per turn while running</b>, <b>+2 when the camp
        develops</b>, and a reset to full when the unit spawns. Nothing
        subtracts turns when a unit dies.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>"It dropped from 7 to 5 after I killed a unit"</b> is the freeze
        ending, not turns being removed: while the camp is at cap the countdown
        is frozen (the tile tooltip appends <em>"(PAUSED)"</em>) and reads 7
        turn after turn. Your kill brought the nearby count below the cap, the
        clock restarted, and two turns of fighting later it reads 5.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Why it sometimes doesn't change:</b> the kill didn't drop the count
        below the cap, or you killed a <em>raider</em> (units out raiding never
        count) — and it can flip with no kills at all, when raiders leave or
        return, units cross the {range}-tile ring, or a nearby city becomes
        raidable (the cap doubles).
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>The develop timer</b> counts up toward the tier's threshold (each
        camp also rolls a random head start at placement, up to 10 turns,
        scaled down at lower Tribal Levels). It <b>pauses while a foreign unit
        stands on the camp tile</b>. On upgrade, the camp starts rolling the
        stronger tier's units and its next-unit timer gets +2 turns.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section">
    <h2 class="cs-h2">The numbers</h2>
    <div class="cs-cols">
      <div>
        <h3 class="cs-sub">By Tribal Level</h3>
        <table class="cs-tbl">
          <thead><tr><th>Tribal Level</th><th>New unit every</th><th>Camp cap bonus</th><th>Raid range</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {camps.levels.map((l: any) => (
              <tr>
                <td>{l.name}</td>
                <td><b>{l.interval}</b> turns</td>
                <td>+{l.defendUnits}</td>
                <td>{l.raidRange > 0 ? `${l.raidRange} tiles` : '—'}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="cs-sub">By camp tier (settlements develop over time)</h3>
        <table class="cs-tbl">
          <thead><tr><th>Camp</th><th>Camp cap base</th><th>Develops in</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {camps.settlements.map((s: any) => (
              <tr>
                <td>{s.name}</td>
                <td>+{s.defendUnits}</td>
                <td>{s.developTurns > 0 ? `${s.developTurns} turns` : 'top tier'}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="cs-note">
      Example at Normal: an Outpost caps at 2+2 = <b>4</b> units within
      {range} tiles — or just <b>2</b> if it has no city to raid in range.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section">
    <h2 class="cs-h2">Corollaries worth knowing</h2>
    <ul class="cs-notes">
      <li><b>Camps near your borders feel relentless by design:</b> with a raidable city in range, they tolerate <b>twice the standing force</b> before pausing.</li>
      <li><b>Raiders don't count.</b> Units that leave to raid stop counting against the cap, so a camp can keep spawning while its warband is out.</li>
      <li><b>Standing on a camp stops its upgrade</b> — the develop timer doesn't tick while a foreign unit occupies the camp tile (the next-unit timer is unaffected by occupation; only the cap governs it).</li>
      <li>The very first countdown of the game is <b>halved on turn 1</b>.</li>
      <li>In co-op multiplayer (all players on one team) intervals scale by 4/(3+players) — more players, faster camps.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <p class="provenance">
    Mechanic transcribed from <code>Tile.doTurn</code> →
    <code>skipImprovementUnitTurns</code> / <code>resetImprovementUnitTurns</code> /
    <code>countTribeAvailableUnits</code> in the game source; intervals, camp
    tiers and Tribal-Level knobs are read from the game's XML each patch.
    The source functions are hash-watched, so a patch that changes the pause
    rule trips this site's drift alarm. See also
    <a href={`${base}tribes`}>Tribes</a>.
  </p>
</Base>

<style>
  .lede {
    font-family: var(--font-display); font-style: italic;
    font-size: 1rem; line-height: 1.55; color: var(--text-soft);
    max-width: 70ch; margin: 0.5rem 0 1.5rem;
  }
  .lede b { color: var(--text); }
  .lede code, .cs-rule code, .provenance code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-style: normal; font-size: 0.82em; color: var(--text-dim);
  }

  .cs-section { margin-bottom: 1.8rem; }
  .cs-h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-display); font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.25rem;
    color: var(--text); margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--gold-deep); padding-bottom: 0.3rem;
  }
  .cs-sub {
    font-family: var(--font-ui); font-size: 0.62rem; text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em; color: var(--gold-soft); font-weight: 500; margin: 0 0 0.4rem;
  }

  .cs-rule {
    font-size: 0.9rem; line-height: 1.65; color: var(--text-soft); max-width: 76ch;
    background: var(--bg-elev); border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--gold-deep); border-radius: 4px;
    padding: 0.75rem 1rem;
  }
  .cs-rule b { color: var(--text); }
  .cs-formula {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.82rem; color: var(--gold-soft);
    background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); border-radius: 3px;
    padding: 0.35rem 0.6rem; margin: 0.5rem 0;
  }
  .cs-rule__mods { margin: 0.2rem 0 0.6rem; padding-left: 1.2rem; }
  .cs-rule__mods li { margin-bottom: 0.15rem; }

  .cs-cols { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(19rem, 1fr)); gap: 1.2rem; }
  .cs-tbl { border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; font-size: 0.84rem; }
  .cs-tbl th {
    font-family: var(--font-ui); font-size: 0.6rem; text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em; color: var(--text-faint); text-align: left;
    padding: 0.25rem 0.7rem 0.25rem 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }
  .cs-tbl td { padding: 0.3rem 0.7rem 0.3rem 0; border-bottom: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--border) 55%, transparent); color: var(--text-soft); }
  .cs-tbl td b { font-family: var(--font-mono); color: var(--text); }
  .cs-note { font-size: 0.82rem; color: var(--text-dim); margin: 0.6rem 0 0; }
  .cs-note b { font-family: var(--font-mono); color: var(--gold-soft); }

  .cs-body { font-size: 0.88rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-soft); max-width: 74ch; margin: 0.3rem 0 0.6rem; }
  .cs-body b { color: var(--text); }
  .cs-notes { margin: 0; padding-left: 1.1rem; max-width: 76ch; }
  .cs-notes li { font-size: 0.86rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-soft); margin-bottom: 0.35rem; }
  .cs-notes b { color: var(--text); }

  .provenance { margin: 1.6rem 0 0; font-size: 0.76rem; color: var(--text-faint); line-height: 1.55; max-width: 80ch; }
  .provenance a { color: var(--gold-soft); }
</style>
